// 8-bit and 64-bit multiply, shuffles, reductions) run a scalar loop over the lanes with identical results; the support
// check happens before an instruction is executed, never after. Comparison methods give a mask vector (all bits set in the
// lanes where it holds); `select` combines two vectors by a mask. Arithmetic wraps like the scalar operators. Floating
// point follows IEEE-754; `sum` adds the lanes from lane 0 to the last one, so its rounding is the scalar loop's.
// `a + b`, `a - b` and `a * b` call `add`, `sub` and `mul`.

